#{[#edb/ref 17592186050555 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125299} :activity/id #uuid "8cf4b4e2-a2a4-5056-88ef-3cbe1bd9eadd" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125299 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125736 :tutorial/content "Let's consider a surface $z=f(x,y)$ and a unit vector $\\mathbf{u} = \\left\\langle  u_1, u_2 \\right\\rangle.$\n\nThe **directional derivative of $f$** at the point $(x_0,y_0)$ in the direction of $\\mathbf{u}$ is\n\n$$\nf'_{\\mathbf u}(x_0,y_0) = \\lim\\limits_{t \\to 0}\\dfrac{f(\\mathbf x_0 + t \\mathbf u) - f(\\mathbf x_0)}{t}.\n$$\n\nThe directional derivative of $f$ is the rate of change of the function $f$ in the direction of $\\mathbf{u}.$ We'll discuss this idea more later in the lesson.\n\nIt turns out that if $f$ is differentiable, we can write $f'_{\\mathbf u}$ in terms of partial derivatives $f_x$ and $f_y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nf'_{\\mathbf u}(x_0,y_0) = \\nabla f (x_0,y_0) \\cdot \\mathbf u.\n$$\n\nAt the end of the lesson, we'll derive this formula. But for now, let's get some practice using it.\n\nFor example, let's find the directional derivative of the function $f(x, y) = x^2 + y^2$ at the point $(x_0,y_0)=(1,2)$ in the direction of the vector $\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j}.$\n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $\\mathbf{u}$ in the direction of $\\mathbf{v} \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ u & =\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i+j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the gradient $\\nabla f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,2)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =2x\\,i+2y\\,j \\\\ ∇\\,f(1,2) & =(2\\cdot 1)\\,i+(2\\cdot 2)\\,j \\\\  & =2i+4j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{'}(1,2) & =∇\\,f(1,2)\\cdot u \\\\  & =(2\\,i+4\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}j) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125300}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125300 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124458 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124262 :question/id #uuid "bf35c502-5a22-5953-9158-b2464d28c09f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3377" :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $g(x, y) =3x^2+4xy$ at the point $A(0,1)$ in the direction towards the point $B(1,4).$" :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative $g'_{\\mathbf u}(a,b)$ of a function $g(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $\\mathbf v$ is given by\n\n$$\ng'_{\\mathbf u}(a,b) = \\nabla g(a,b)\\cdot\\mathbf u\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $\\mathbf v.$\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector and the corresponding unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨1,4⟩-⟨0,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,3⟩ \\\\ u & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{‖\\overset{\\to }{AB}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}}}⟨1,3⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨1,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the gradient $\\nabla g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $A(0,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}+4xy),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}+4xy)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6x+4y,\\,4x⟩ \\\\ ∇g(0,1) & =⟨6\\cdot 0+4\\cdot 1,\\,4\\cdot 0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{u}^{′}(0,1) & =∇g(0,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨4,0⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}},\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Directional Derivative"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125301}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125301 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125737 :tutorial/content "From the formula for the directional derivative, we get that if  $z = f (x, y)$ is a differentiable function and $\\mathbf u$ is a unit vector, then\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\nf'_{\\mathbf u} &= \\nabla f \\cdot \\mathbf u \\[3pt]\n                      &= |\\nabla f| |\\mathbf u| \\cos\\theta\\[3pt]\n                      & = |\\nabla f| \\cos\\theta,\n}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta$ is the angle between the vectors $\\nabla f$ and $\\mathbf u,$ as depicted below.\n\n![](images/50/50c82908d5fd619c1c3c45f2aa3ff26929b8f369233c08d0688a091d2d3c123c.png)\n\nIt follows that $f'_{\\mathbf u}$ is maximized when $\\theta =0.$ In other words, $f'_{\\mathbf u}$ is maximized when $\\mathbf u$ and $\\nabla f$ point in the same direction. Thus, the maximum value of $f'_{\\mathbf u}$ is $|\\nabla f|.$\n\nA similar argument shows that $f'_{\\mathbf u}$ reaches its minimum value $- |\\nabla f|$ when $\\theta = \\pi;$ that is, when $\\nabla f$ and $\\mathbf u$ point in opposite directions.\n\nAs a result, we get the following theorem:\n\n> *A differentiable function $f$ increases most rapidly in the direction of the gradient and decreases most rapidly in the opposite direction to that of its gradient.*\n\nFor functions of more than two variables, the same theorem holds." :tutorial/title "The Gradient as the Direction of Maximum Increase of a Function"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125302}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125302 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124459 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124263 :question/id #uuid "dc1e4548-ce72-5618-86f4-b68b46b50550" :question/math-academy-id "e-3381" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f (x, y) = \\ln (x^2y)$ decrease most rapidly at the point $(2, 1)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the direction opposite to that of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $-\\nabla f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇f(x,y) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}y))\\text{ }i-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}y))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}\\cdot 2xy\\text{ }i-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}\\cdot {x}^{2}j \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{x}\\text{ }i-\\frac{1}{y}\\text{ }j \\\\ -∇f(2,1) & =-\\frac{2}{2}\\text{ }i-\\frac{1}{1}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function decreases most rapidly in the direction of $- \\, \\mathbf i - \\mathbf j.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Direction of Maximum Increase or Maximum Decrease of a Function"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125304}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125303 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124460 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124264 :question/id #uuid "1eea4610-508a-5437-802e-9d009b777e82" :question/math-academy-id "e-3382" :question/problem "Consider the function $g (x, y) = \\ln \\left(\\sqrt {xy^3}\\right).$ What is the rate of change of $g$ at the point $\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)$ in the direction in which the function increases most rapidly?" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $\\nabla g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)\\!\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}})),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{2\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}},\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}}\\cdot \\frac{3x{y}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2x},\\,\\frac{3}{2y}⟩ \\\\ ∇g(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}) & =⟨\\frac{1}{2\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})},\\frac{3}{2\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2})}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)$ in the direction of $\\nabla g\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖∇g(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2})‖ & =‖⟨1,1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rate of Change of a Function in a Given Direction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125305}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125304 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125738 :tutorial/content "For a surface $z = f(x,y),$ the partial derivative $f_x(x_0,y_0)$ gives us the rate of change of $f$ in the direction of the vector $\\mathbf{i} = \\langle 1,0\\rangle.$ But this is just a special case of the directional derivative, because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{i}^{'}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}) & =∇({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{f}_{x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),{f}_{y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})⟩\\cdot ⟨1,0⟩ \\\\  & ={f}_{x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{j}^{'}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}) & =∇({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨0,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{f}_{x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),{f}_{y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})⟩\\cdot ⟨0,1⟩ \\\\  & ={f}_{y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the directional derivative generalizes the notion of a partial derivative. \n\nNow, recall that the partial derivatives $f_x$ and $f_y$ represent the rate of change of $f$ in the direction of vectors $\\mathbf i$ and $\\mathbf j,$ respectively. As for a general directional derivative, we obtain the following property:\n\n> *The directional derivative $f'_\\mathbf{u}(x_0,y_0)$ represents the rate of change of the function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(x_0,y_0)$ in the direction of $\\mathbf{u}.$*" :tutorial/title "The Directional Derivative as a Generalization of the Partial Derivative"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125503}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125305 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125739 :tutorial/content "Consider a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ and suppose that the point $P$ with coordinates $(x_0,y_0, f(x_0,y_0))$ lies on $S.$ We wish to derive a formula for the directional derivative $f'_\\mathbf{u}(x_0,y_0),$ where $\\mathbf u = \\langle u_1, u_2 \\rangle$ is a unit vector.\n\nGeometrically, $f'_{\\mathbf u}(x_0,y_0)$ can be constructed in the following way:\n\n- Define a vertical plane $\\Pi$ that's parallel to $\\mathbf u$ and passes through $P.$\n\n- Define the curve $C$ as the intersection of the surface $S$ and the plane $\\Pi.$\n\n- Then, the directional derivative $f'_{\\mathbf u}$ is the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$\n\nThe geometric interpretation of $f'_{\\mathbf u}$ is similar to that of the partial derivatives $f_x$ and $f_y.$ The only difference here is that we are not restricting the plane $\\Pi$ to be parallel to the coordinate axes.\n\nNow, since $f'_{\\mathbf u}$ is tangent to $C$ and is in the direction of $\\mathbf u$ at $P,$ the vector \n\n$$\n\\mathbf v = \\langle u_1, u_2, f'_{\\mathbf u}\\rangle\n$$\n\nis tangent to our surface $S$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/59/59cb8fa410b9194c298d8f477ab0d0cff3e27765bc45c14cb9b5ea9210288184.png)\n\nRecall that $\\langle f_x (x_0,y_0), f_y(x_0,y_0), -1\\rangle$ is known to be a normal vector to the surface at $P.$ Since this vector must be perpendicular to $\\mathbf v,$ the dot product of the two vectors must be zero, which means that\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\langle u_1, u_2, f'_{\\mathbf u}\\rangle \\cdot \\langle f_x (x_0,y_0), f_y(x_0,y_0), -1\\rangle &=0 \\[5pt]\nu_1 f_x (x_0,y_0) + u_2 f_y (x_0,y_0) - f'_{\\mathbf u} &=0 \\[5pt]\nf'_{\\mathbf u} = \\nabla f (x_0,y_0) \\cdot \\mathbf u. }\n$$\n\nWe can interpret this formula as the projection of $\\nabla f (x_0,y_0)$ onto the direction of $\\mathbf u,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/82/82e7dece2597e15172600ff598090241facb834a7248ed54286fa777c25a6ebd.png)" :tutorial/title "Deriving the Formula For the Directional Derivative"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125503 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125825 :tutorial/content "What is the directional derivative of the function $\\mathbf{f}$ at $(x_0,y_0)$ in the direction of the gradient? \n\nLet's apply our formula. First, we find the unit vector $\\mathbf{u}$ in the direction of $\\nabla\\! f\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{∇\\,f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})}{‖∇\\,f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})‖}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{'}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}) & =∇\\,f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot \\frac{∇\\,f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})}{‖∇\\,f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{∇\\,f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ∇\\,f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})}{‖∇\\,f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{‖∇\\,f({x}_{0},{y}_{0}){‖}^{2}}{‖∇\\,f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})‖} \\\\  & =‖∇\\,f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude the following:\n\n> *The directional derivative in the direction of the gradient equals the norm of the gradient. Furthermore, the norm of the gradient can be interpreted as the rate of change of the function in the direction of the gradient.*" :tutorial/title "The Rate of Change in the Direction of the Gradient"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125303}}] :activity/title "Directional Derivatives" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124730}] [#edb/ref 17592186050560 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134877} :activity/id #uuid "51afd837-2e82-5c9f-a65c-d3df7260a455" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134830 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135109 :tutorial/content "To calculate the circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nFor example, consider the region $D$ below.\n\n![](images/6d/6d7573de141b9a640242644c64fc79a002d93b595fc0d05d1e9ad75317358cb3.png)\n\nSuppose that $\\mathbf F$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$ defined over $D,$ and\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r = 12, \n\\qquad \n\\oint\\limits_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r = -8, \n\\qquad \n\\oint\\limits_{C_3}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r = -2,\n$$\n\nwhere $C_1$ is oriented counterclockwise, and $C_2,$ and $C_3$ are oriented clockwise. Let's use this information to calculate the value of the circulation $\\Gamma,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\Gamma = \\displaystyle\\oint\\limits_{\\partial D}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r.\n$$\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/ab/ab13029d8b9168b384e14d2ae5f10d63c80b3fc8ca46695f59c2405443fa6b14.png)\n\nSince the value of each line integral is given for a positively oriented curve, we can calculate the total circulation over $D$ by summing the circulations over each boundary curve. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ & ={∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =12+(-8)+(-2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Calculating a Circulation Over a Region Containing Holes"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134870}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134836 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133886 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133702 :question/id #uuid "d6e8fac3-5dfc-5487-bd81-dc33aeae5f1c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9097" :question/problem "![](images/83/83bd636f3a294c641e171c5a8281aaa7068e1931a06229064314e8f96efd0c3a.png)\n\nCalculate ![](images/c4/c4d83a5e5d029ef5f5e06b68d3cc39ea72ecf0c404f18d6a0ded750b0e2a6f39.svg) where ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) is the annular region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let ![](images/c0/c0c348883f19c18a48444415da35aeaa27f8a18606bf5955e34aec416e248cad.svg) be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) be the region bounded by ![](images/27/278f99df1e14fe0ebfb61bdb72262a62670dcaa60d322148bc3ee591b278ec2d.svg) If ![](images/1a/1aaddf0d4be4d873ff6c0600b0b239e0565a05ca8daaff7ca92bd5c70bebcf03.svg) and ![](images/4b/4be111afe7c23428ae5024a8bb9424de4d5b9e8cce74d2258fdffb9bdca9bcfd.svg) have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing ![](images/87/87e7e0743ecd6609730f4b9a9b3681b1f360ca86bcb209e03561a197b18782f3.svg) then Green's theorem states that\n![](images/ac/accf185e064f4c9c99cc6ad1437aabc7d5471cc332ff6c38d51a3f11ec6e26f2.svg)\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that ![](images/09/090c7fe781ec77412a904bb44dda28812c8a4e9a8524886e36041ffcabe9f0a4.svg) and ![](images/d7/d719ec6c4bb88290d50d81fc268cc438cb3002659b6ac79210889d779a09d44c.svg) So,\n\n![](images/94/94e9af3cff89c9bcf412776abcab67d1852ba083401829edcd746cb19311c8b9.svg)\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n![](images/ec/ec5ca2469ae1fd23385b27e906cb952f6b07c2bbfe02acbc42afa20284d4a122.svg)"} :knowledge-point/title "Applying Green's Theorem to Regions With Holes"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134873}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134870 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133899 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133715 :question/id #uuid "18eef985-0fd4-5b08-8072-684ded227ab4" :question/math-academy-id "e-9557" :question/problem "![](images/16/16026d4ac7deeb30c1abb3a066df23a06a544116463b364b0a1e9e0bac0608c8.png)\n\nSuppose that $\\mathbf F$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r = 10, \n\\qquad \n\\oint\\limits_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r = -8, \n\\qquad \n\\oint\\limits_{C_3}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r = 3,\n$$\n\nwhere $C_1$ and $C_3$ are oriented clockwise, and $C_2$ is oriented counterclockwise, calculate the value of $\\displaystyle\\oint\\limits_{\\partial D}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r.$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve $C_1$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse $C_1$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves $C_2$ and $C_3$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse $C_2$ and $C_3$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/11/11445c083440ee2fde1864d599a8bbd68146d522c61de11b3e90dc231e6eddf1.png)\n\nNotice that the positive orientations of the outer curve $C_1$ and the inner curve $C_2$ are opposite those stated in the question.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =-10-(-8)+3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Circulation of a Vector Field Over a Region Containing Holes"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134872}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134871 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133900 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133716 :question/id #uuid "defe326f-f578-568c-a9b3-3d01f67da058" :question/math-academy-id "e-9558" :question/problem "![](images/64/64ba92b021de2ba2f4fc98f1ce76fa0d16b3c8b7ed9ae45237cb8772b111c8b8.png)\n\n  \n\nCalculate \n$\\displaystyle\n\\oint\\limits_{\\partial D} (2x^3y^2-x^5) \\,\\mathrm{d}x + (3x^4y+y^4) \\,\\mathrm{d}y,$\nwhere $D$ is the region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y \n= \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2x^3y^2-x^5$ and $Q(x,y)= 3x^4y+y^4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 4x^3y, \\qquad \n\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = 12x^3y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}(2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-{x}^{5})\\,dx+(3{x}^{4}y+{y}^{4})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}12{x}^{3}y-4{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}8{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in Cartesian coordinates as $\\Delta_1\\setminus\\Delta_2,$ where\n\n- $\\Delta_1 = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: -2 \\leq x \\leq 1, -1 \\leq y \\leq 2 \\big\\}$ is the region enclosed by the outer curve, and\n\n- $\\Delta_2 = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: -1 \\leq x \\leq 0, 0 \\leq y \\leq 1 \\big\\}$ is the region enclosed by the inner curve.\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}8{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{{Δ}_{1}}8{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}A-{∬}_{{Δ}_{2}}8{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{1}8{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}8{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{1}4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{-1}^{2}2y\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{-1}^{0}4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{1}2y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={x}^{4}\\,{|}_{-2}^{1}\\cdot {y}^{2}\\,{|}_{-1}^{2}-{x}^{4}\\,{|}_{-1}^{0}\\cdot {y}^{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(1-16)\\cdot (4-1)-(0-1)\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =(-15)\\cdot 3-(-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-44.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Applying Green's Theorem to Regions With Holes: Advanced Cases"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134879}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134872 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135133 :tutorial/content "We now know how to calculate the circulation of a vector field $\\mathbf F$ over a region $D$ that contains holes. \n\nIn previous lessons, we've seen how to use Green's theorem to calculate circulation. Often, Green's theorem allows us to convert a complex line integral over $\\partial D$ to a double integral over $D.$\n\nSo the question is, can we use Green's theorem to calculate the circulation of $\\mathbf F$ over $\\partial D$ when $D$ contains holes?\n\nAs it turns out, the answer is yes. Moreover, we can show that applying Green's theorem to regions containing holes works the same way as with regions that don't have holes. We'll describe this in more detail at the end of the lesson.\n\nWe can summarize Green's theorem to regions containing holes in a single picture, shown below. \n\n![](images/37/379238518375618b14a550ef571564ae4fa74238c9b112e80c559c6e54e74eec.png)\n\nNote that the region $D$ can contain a finite number of holes." :tutorial/title "Extending Green's Theorem to Regions With Holes"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134878}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134873 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135134 :tutorial/content "Let's suppose that we want to calculate the double integral\n\n$$\n\\iint_\\limits{D} f(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region shown below. \n\n![](images/65/65f622409e3fdd43f56dde79513e8693751465bb131dd828ce9f6cdfaa7d48aa.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the region $D$ can be represented as the difference of sets $D = \\Delta_1\\setminus\\Delta_2,$ where\n\n- $\\Delta_1 = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: -3 \\leq x \\leq 4, -2 \\leq y \\leq 3 \\big\\}$ is the region enclosed by the outer curve, and\n\n- $\\Delta_2 = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 1 \\leq x \\leq 3, 1 \\leq y \\leq 2 \\big\\}$ is the region enclosed by the inner curve.\n\nIn such cases, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{{Δ}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A-{∬}_{{Δ}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nIn other words, we can calculate a double integral over a region with a hole as the difference between two integrals." :tutorial/title "A Note About Double Integrals Over Regions With Holes"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134871}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134877 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135138 :tutorial/content "Suppose we wish to calculate the circulation $\\Gamma$ of a vector field $\\mathbf F$ over the boundary of the region $D$ shown below. \n\n![](images/35/35a04c813c0b6922c6ee1a71997b9ca0c01a532dde274ca84165cfd97f511201.png)\n\nNotice that $D$ is the region between the closed curves $C_1$ and $C_2,$ and has a hole. \n\nWhen dealing with more complex regions, we usually write $\\partial D$ to refer to the boundary of $D.$ So, in this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\partial D = C_1 \\cup C_2.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe circulation of $\\mathbf F$ over the boundary of $D$ is simply the sum of the circulations over the two bounding curves:\n\n$$\n\\Gamma \n= \\oint\\limits_{\\partial D} \\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d\\mathbf r\n=\n\\oint\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d\\mathbf r\n+\n\\oint\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\nNotice that we have not yet determined the orientations of $C_1$ and $C_2.$ For this, we note the following important points:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve $C_1$ because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse $C_1$ counterclockwise, and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curve $C_2$ because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse $C_2$ clockwise.\n\nTherefore, to apply the above formula for the circulation, we need to choose our orientations as follows:\n\n![](images/54/5496ce5a457601608d5345f4176dc918b12ac090d5f8a77d8a834986352d3064.png)" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134830}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134878 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135139 :tutorial/content "To calculate the circulation of a vector field over a region $D$ containing holes, we add together the circulations over each positively oriented boundary. The outer curves must be oriented counterclockwise for this to work, whereas the inner curves must be oriented clockwise.\n\nTo make sense of why we need to choose our orientations in this way, we turn to Green's theorem. \n\nRecall that Green's theorem tells us that the macroscopic circulation over $\\partial D$ equals the sum of the scalar curls inside $D.$\n\n![](images/37/379238518375618b14a550ef571564ae4fa74238c9b112e80c559c6e54e74eec.png)\n\nNow, if we exaggerate the size of two of the curls inside $D,$ we get the following picture:\n\n![](images/5a/5aa0a1f7a3b021ac85984b8af188a83da5e4f990c4a188676a90ef6d1cf45a61.png)\n\nNow, notice the following:\n\n- For the curls' direction to match with the orientation of the *outer* curve at the points where they are in close contact, the outer curve must be oriented *counterclockwise*.\n\n- Similarly, for the curls' direction to match with the orientation of the *inner* curve at the points where they are in close contact, the inner curve must be oriented *clockwise*.\n\nSo, for Green's theorem to give us the correct answer, the inner and outer curves must be oriented clockwise and counterclockwise, respectively." :tutorial/title "Intuition Behind the Choice of Orentations"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134836}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134879 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135140 :tutorial/content "When proving Green's theorem, we typically start by assuming that $D$ is a so-called **simple region** (that is, a region that is both type I and type II). Then, Green's theorem is *extended* to include regions that are not simple.\n\nLet's suppose that we've proved that Green's theorem is true for type I and type II regions. How can we use this to show that Green's theorem is also true for regions containing holes?\n\nTo illustrate how this is done, let's consider the vector field $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j,$ and the line integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\oint\\limits_{\\partial D} \\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $D$ is the annular region below.\n\n![](images/57/57c22c3d7a92ce4a1a1a798f7e6a861ded2e9a6be4d8d98c36869fe29e2783d0.png)\n\nNote that $\\partial D= C_1\\cup C_2$ is positively oriented. \n\nNow, imagine splitting $D$ into two regions $D_1$ and $D_2$ by means of *cuts* along the segments $[-4, -2]$ and $[2,4]$ on the $x$-axis, as shown below \n\n![](images/e7/e7f0d41c3fb909073cdaf4ea23f4ab617032e0d24bdc8d9b9e3724e7d56abe3b.png)\n\nNotice that $D_1$ and $D_2$ are positively oriented type I regions. In addition, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1} & ={C}_{3}\\cup {C}_{8} \\\\ {C}_{2} & ={C}_{5}\\cup {C}_{10} \\\\ ∂{D}_{1} & ={C}_{3}\\cup {C}_{4}\\cup {C}_{5}\\cup {C}_{6}, \\\\ ∂{D}_{2} & ={C}_{8}\\cup (-{C}_{6})\\cup {C}_{10}\\cup (-{C}_{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the circulation of $\\mathbf F$ over $\\partial D$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}\\cup \\,{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{8}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{5}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{10}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & +{{{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{-{C}_{4}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{6}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{-{C}_{6}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r}_{⏟}}_{0} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{5}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{6}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & +{\\int }_{{C}_{8}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{-{C}_{6}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{10}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{-{C}_{4}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={∮}_{∂{D}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{∂{D}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={∮}_{∂{D}_{1}}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y+{∮}_{∂{D}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{{D}_{1}}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{{D}_{2}}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTo summarize,\n\n$$\n\\oint \\limits_{\\partial D}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = \\iint_\\limits{D}\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is just Green's theorem for the region $D.$" :tutorial/title "An Illustration of How To Extend Green’s Theorem to Regions Containing Holes"}}] :activity/title "Extending Green's Theorem" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133998}] [#edb/ref 17592186050534 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134922} :activity/id #uuid "9c33b9fa-2311-5a26-bf22-2344e0c3157a" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134922 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135160 :tutorial/content "Consider a two-dimensional vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ on $\\mathbb R^2$ with component functions $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ defined over a region $D.$ \n\nRecall that Green's theorem in flux form states that the sum of the microscopic \"fluxes\" (divergences) of $\\mathbf{F}$ inside $D$ equals the macroscopic flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across the boundary of $D.$ \n\nWe can express Green's theorem in flux form as a single picture, shown below.\n\n![](images/52/52191ef5fea6e08b885dfd9ffea547e3843b8113847b532f8b4aa5d966d15213.png)\n\n    \n\nWe'll now state the higher-dimensional analog of Green's theorem in flux form. Please note that we will restrict our attention to so-called **simple regions** for the remainder of this lesson. Simple regions in $\\mathbb R^3$ can be classified as type I, type II, *and* type III." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134939}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134933 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133929 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133489 :question/id #uuid "03c75b64-65f4-5e12-b7b1-fa8b61893eb7" :question/math-academy-id "e-12447" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $x=-1,$ $x=1,$ $y=0,$ $y=1,$ and the surfaces $z=y$ and $z=1+x^2+y^2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F} = (x+z )\\, \\mathbf{i} + yz \\, \\mathbf{j} + x^2y \\, \\mathbf{k},$ express the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ as a triple integral, and then as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n= \\iiint\\limits_R \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\: \\textrm{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+z)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(yz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}y) \\\\  & =1+z+0 \\\\  & =z+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iiint\\limits_R (z+1) \\: \\textrm{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type I solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\:-1 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\: y \\leq z \\leq 1+x^2+y^2 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nExpressing our triple integral as a repeated integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}z+1\\,\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,z+1\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Flux Across a Closed Surface as a Triple Integral"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134934}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134934 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133930 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133490 :question/id #uuid "9bb760a6-9060-532a-a59d-3cedf335daac" :question/math-academy-id "e-12448" :question/problem "Consider the positively oriented surface $S$ given by $x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = 4$ and the vector field $\\mathbf{F} = 2x \\, \\mathbf{i} + y \\, \\mathbf{j} + 4 \\, \\mathbf{k}.$ Use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n= \\iiint\\limits_R \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\: \\textrm{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4) \\\\  & =2+1+0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid sphere of radius $2$ enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}3\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =3{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\pi \\cdot {2}^{3} \\\\  & =32\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across the Surface of a Known Solid"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134935}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134935 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133931 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133491 :question/id #uuid "dfba5156-6b5f-5622-9406-7598ae2630b6" :question/math-academy-id "e-12449" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=1,$ $y=0,$ $y=2,$ $z=1,$ $z=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F} =e^z \\, \\mathbf{i} + x^2y^2 \\, \\mathbf{j} + 4x^2yz \\, \\mathbf{k},$ find the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n= \\iiint\\limits_R \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\: \\textrm{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{z})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4{x}^{2}yz) \\\\  & =0+2{x}^{2}y+4{x}^{2}y \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}2y\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{3}]_{x=0}^{x=1}\\cdot [{y}^{2}]_{y=0}^{y=2}\\cdot (2-1) \\\\  & =(1-0)\\cdot (4-0)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across a Closed Surface"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134936 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135164 :tutorial/content "Consider the vector field \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F} = \\langle 2x+1,\\: 3z,\\: xy \\rangle\n$$\n\nand the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown below. \n\n![](images/cf/cf90197ac99a066db75154200de4cbee2494fef15deed6be7b7c4cd501ae8f26.png)\n\n \n\nLet's find the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S.$\n\nTo find the total flux directly, we would need to find the flux through each of the six surfaces of our rectangular solid and then add the results. However, using the divergence theorem\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n= \\iiint\\limits_R \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\: \\textrm{d}V,\n$$\n\nwe can reduce this computation to a single triple integral.\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+1)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3z)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy) \\\\  & =2+0+0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux through $S$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}2\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =2{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Calculating the Flux Across a Surface"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134933}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134939 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135170 :tutorial/content "The **divergence theorem** is a higher-dimensional analog of Green's theorem in flux form. It states the following:\n\n*Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then*\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_R \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\: \\textrm{d}V\n=\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathbf{n}\\:\\mathrm{d}S\n$$\n\n*where $\\mathbf{n}$ is the outward-pointing unit normal to $S.$*\n\nIn other words, the sum of the microscopic \"fluxes\" (divergences) of $\\mathbf{F}$ inside $R$ equals the macroscopic flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ through the boundary of $R.$ \n\nThe divergence theorem can be summarized in a single diagram.\n\n![](images/ff/ffabcceb5f4fd520a57f909c476b267f584ac4dc3fb948a5c1996b71dbc74e6f.png)\n\nThe divergence theorem can be used to simplify flux calculations. Calculating the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across a surface can be time-consuming because we often need to consider the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across multiple faces that make up $S.$ The divergence theorem often allows us to reduce a complicated flux calculation to a single triple integral." :tutorial/title "The Divergence Theorem"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134936}}] :activity/title "The Divergence Theorem" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134012}] [#edb/ref 17592186050567 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134864} :activity/id #uuid "80a63c78-ab52-5147-b1df-b7736290c5a2" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134864 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135131 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $\\mathbf F =P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2,$ and a region $D$ is bounded by a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve $C,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/fa/fa3efbc9200a6acdda01e503999f7588cd44fd63af77fa0a6abf1b5303c9a889.png)\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's now consider what happens when the scalar curl term on the left-hand side of the above equation equals unity everywhere on $D.$ In other words, let's consider what happens when\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=1.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the double integral gives the area of $D,$ because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=\\text{Area}(D).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThus, we've just discovered a new method of computing the area of $D.$ We simply choose a vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)$ whose scalar curl equals unity everywhere on $D,$ and then compute its circulation over $C.$\n\nThere are infinitely many vector fields that we can use to compute the area of a region. Some of the most common for this purpose are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)= \\left\\langle -y, \\: 0 \\right\\rangle, \n\\qquad\n\\mathbf{G}(x,y)= \\left\\langle -\\dfrac{y}{2}, \\: \\dfrac{x}{2} \\right\\rangle,\n\\qquad\n\\mathbf{H}(x,y)= \\left\\langle 0, \\: x \\right\\rangle\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134868}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134865 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133895 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133711 :question/id #uuid "a817c3c6-97bb-55e4-9a1e-04ed99d3eaab" :question/math-academy-id "e-9553" :question/problem "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ which of the following could be the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)?$\n\nI. $\\left\\langle -y, \\: y\\right\\rangle$\n\nII. $\\left\\langle -2x, \\: 2x\\right\\rangle$\n\nIII. $\\left\\langle 5y\\sin x, \\: x-5\\cos x \\right\\rangle$" :question/worked-solution "According to Green's theorem, if $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = \\left\\langle P(x,y), \\: Q(x,y) \\right\\rangle,$ where\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Vector field I gives the required area. In this case, \n$\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = -1$\nand\n$\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = 0,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field II does not give the required area. In this case, \n$\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 0$\nand\n$\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = 2,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 2.\n$$\n\n- Vector field III gives the required area. Notice that\n$\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 5\\sin x$\nand\n$\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = 1+5\\sin x,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields That Satisfy the Unit Curl Property"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134866}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134866 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133896 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133712 :question/id #uuid "74c9ecfb-5005-53ff-b7a9-db798488587b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9554" :question/problem "Let $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\langle 2\\sin t, \\: 2 \\cos t \\rangle$ for $t \\in \\left [0, 2\\pi \\right).$ \nThe area of $D$ can be written as\n\n![](images/45/45513e4aedd37b868d2020cbbb47f49938f6ca03487ded8b5e519a2ec8e4edf5.svg)\n\nwhere $P(x,y) = -y$ and $Q(x,y) = 0.$ Determine the missing part of the equation.\n\n*Hint: Note that $C$ is negatively oriented.*\n\n![](images/a5/a5eeb5db90f9ee0d86a49e5bbf02629c7886fa019db7cff3c4d0e1eb43e8fd75.png)" :question/worked-solution "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 0 - \\left(-1\\right) = 1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since the path is negatively oriented.\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y) = -y$ and $Q(x,y) = 0,$ we get\n\n$$\n- \\oint\\limits_C P \\: \\text{d}x + Q \\: \\text{d}y = \\oint\\limits_C y \\: \\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}'(t) = \\left\\langle  2\\cos t ,\\:  -2 \\sin t \\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing expression is $4\\cos^2 t.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Area Bounded by a Closed Curve Using Green’s Theorem"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134867}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134867 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133897 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133713 :question/id #uuid "aa4400ff-09b0-596c-9857-c13881b72f73" :question/math-academy-id "e-9555" :question/problem "Consider the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C=C_1 \\cup C_2,$ where \n\n- $C_1$ is parametrized by $\\mathbf{r}_1(t) =-2 t^4 \\: \\mathbf{i} + t^3 \\: \\mathbf{j}$ for $t \\in \\left[-1,1\\right],$ and\n\n- $C_2$ is parametrized by $\\mathbf{r}_2(t) = -2\\: \\mathbf{i} -t \\: \\mathbf{j}$ for $t \\in \\left[-1,1\\right].$\n\nUse Green's theorem to find the area of the region.\n\n![](images/14/147e8fdd97fc938ae5482b70d7215483956a860ee3ce69ae5bbebd111dcc343b.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, let $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$\n\nOur simple, piecewise-smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 0 - \\left(-1\\right) = 1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y) = -y$ and $Q(x,y) = 0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C P \\: \\text{d}x + Q \\: \\text{d}y = -\\oint\\limits_C y \\: \\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivatives of $\\mathbf r_1(t)$ and $\\mathbf r_2(t)$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\, & {r}_{1}^{′}(t)=-8{t}^{3}\\,i+3{t}^{2}\\,j \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\, & {r}_{2}^{′}(t)=-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}}y\\,dx \\\\  & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}}y\\,dx-{∮}_{{C}_{2}}y\\,dx \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{3}\\cdot (-8{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-t)\\cdot 0\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{6}\\,\\text{d}t+0 \\\\  & =8[\\frac{{t}^{7}}{7}]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{2}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Area Using Green's Theorem"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134868 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135132 :tutorial/content "As a concrete example, let $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C,$ parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\langle 2\\cos t, \\: \\sin t \\rangle, \\qquad t \\in \\left [0, 2\\pi \\right).\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and its positively oriented bounding curve $C$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/a6/a6d9fe9cad4ccee56d5d61457c475b41371e0705f1aeccd3a18fb4a3fa8e127c.png)\n\nTo calculate the area of $D$ using Green's theorem, we need to find a vector field whose scalar curl equals unity everywhere on $D.$ So, let's choose\n\n$$\nP(x,y) = -y, \\qquad Q(x,y) = 0.\n$$\n\nIndeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 0 - \\left(-1\\right) = 1. \\qquad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $C$ is a positively oriented path, the area of $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting our expressions for $P$ and $Q$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C P \\: \\text{d}x + Q \\: \\text{d}y = -\\oint\\limits_C y \\: \\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's now evaluate the integral using the usual methods. The derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r'}(t) = \\langle -2\\sin t, \\: \\cos t \\rangle.\n$$\n\nSo we can calculate our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }2{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }2(\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2})\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={[t-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =2\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(D)  =2\\pi.\n$$" :tutorial/title "A Concrete Example"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134876}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134876 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135137 :tutorial/content "We've established that if a region $D$ is bounded by a positively oriented curve $C,$ and we select a vector field $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ such that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} =  1,\n$$\n\nthen the area of $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(D) =  \\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = \\oint\\limits_C P \\: \\text{d}x + Q \\: \\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNow, if $C$ is *negatively* oriented, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(D) =  -\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = -\\oint\\limits_C P \\: \\text{d}x + Q \\: \\text{d}y.\n$$" :tutorial/title "Negatively Oriented Curves"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134865}}] :activity/title "Using Green's Theorem to Calculate Area" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134040}]}
